The function `MOBI_RET mobi_decode_infl` is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks. 

The function does not check the length of the input string `rule` before copying it into the `decoded` buffer. This can lead to a buffer overflow if the input string is longer than the size of the `decoded` buffer. 

Additionally, the function does not check if the `decoded_size` pointer is null before dereferencing it, which can lead to a segmentation fault if the pointer is null.

The function also does not check if the `decoded` buffer is null before dereferencing it, which can lead to a segmentation fault if the buffer is null.

The function uses `memmove` to copy data from the `decoded` buffer to the `decoded` buffer, but it does not check if the source and destination pointers are valid before calling `memmove`. This can lead to a buffer overflow if the source or destination pointer is null.

The function uses `debug_print` to print error messages, but it does not check if the `decoded` buffer is null before dereferencing it. This can lead to a segmentation fault if the buffer is null.

The function uses `return MOBI_DATA_CORRUPT` to indicate an error, but it does not check if the `decoded` buffer is null before dereferencing it. This can lead to a segmentation fault if the buffer is null.

To fix these vulnerabilities, the function should be modified to check the length of the input string, null check the pointers, and validate the buffer before copying data into it. 

Here is an example of how the function could be modified to fix these vulnerabilities:

```c
MOBI_RET mobi_decode_infl(unsigned char *decoded, int *decoded_size, const unsigned char *rule) {
    if (decoded == NULL || decoded_size == NULL || rule == NULL) {
        return MOBI_DATA_CORRUPT;
    }

    int pos = *decoded_size;
    char mod = 'i';
    char dir = '<';
    char olddir;
    unsigned char c;
    int rule_len = strlen(rule);

    if (rule_len > INDX_INFLBUF_SIZEMAX) {
        return MOBI_DATA_CORRUPT;
    }

    while ((c = *rule++)) {
        if (c <= 4) {
            mod = (c <= 2) ? 'i' : 'd'; /* insert, delete */
            olddir = dir;
            dir = (c & 2) ? '<' : '>'; /* left, right */
            if (olddir != dir && olddir) {
                pos = (c & 2) ? *decoded_size : 0;
            }
        }
        else if (c > 10 && c < 20) {
            if (dir == '>') {
                pos = *decoded_size;
            }
            pos -= c - 10;
            dir = 0;
        }
        else {
            if (mod == 'i') {
                const unsigned char *s = decoded + pos;
                unsigned char *d = decoded + pos + 1;
                const int l = *decoded_size - pos;
                if (pos < 0 || l < 0 || d + l > decoded + INDX_INFLBUF_SIZEMAX) {
                    debug_print("Out of buffer in %s at pos: %i\n", decoded, pos);
                    return MOBI_DATA_CORRUPT;
                }
                memmove(d, s, (size_t) l);
                decoded[pos] = c;
                (*decoded_size)++;
                if (dir == '>') { pos++; }
            } else {
                if (dir == '<') { pos--; }
                const unsigned char *s = decoded + pos + 1;
                unsigned char *d = decoded + pos;
                const int l = *decoded_size - pos;
                if (pos < 0 || l < 0 || s + l > decoded + INDX_INFLBUF_SIZEMAX) {
                    debug_print("Out of buffer in %s at pos: %i\n", decoded, pos);
                    return MOBI_DATA_CORRUPT;
                }
                if (decoded[pos] != c) {
                    debug_print("Character mismatch in %s at pos: %i (%c != %c)\n", decoded, pos, decoded[pos], c);
                    return MOBI_DATA_CORRUPT;
                }
                memmove(d, s, (size_t) l);
                (*decoded_size)--;
            }
        }
    }
    return MOBI_SUCCESS;
}
